roximation. Proc.NeurIPS. Cited by: From Reward Weighting to Advantage Weighting. F. Wang and Z. Yu (2025) Coefficients-preserving sampling for reinforcement learning with flow matching. arXiv preprint. Cited by: Introduction, Comparison with the DiTAR-GRPO baseline.. H. Wang, B. Tian, W. Li, X. Lv, H. Zhao, and X. Li (2026) FlowTTS-grpo: online reinforcement learning with multi-objective reward optimization for flow-matching based text-to-speech. arXiv preprint. Cited by: Introduction. R. J. Williams (1992) Simple statistical gradient-following algorithms for connectionist reinforcement learning. Machine learning 8 (3), pp. 229–256. Cited by: From Reward Weighting to Advantage Weighting. S. Xue, C. Ge, S. Zhang, Y. Li, and Z. Ma (2025) Advantage weighted matching: aligning rl with pretraining in diffusion models. arXiv preprint. Cited by: Introduction. A. Yang, A. Li, B. Yang, B. Zhang, B. Hui, B. Zheng, B. Yu, C. Gao, C. Huang, C. Lv, et al. (2025) Qwen3 technical report. arXiv preprint. Cited by: Pre-training Configurations.. Q. Yu, Z. Zhang, R. Zhu, Y. Yuan, X. Zuo, Y. Yue, W. Dai, T. Fan, G. Liu, L. Liu, et al. (2026) Dapo: an open-source llm reinforcement learning system at scale. Proc.NeurIPS. Cited by: From Exponential to Linear Group-Relative Advantage. H. Zen, V. Dang, R. Clark, Y. Zhang, R. J. Weiss, Y. Jia, Z. Chen, and Y. Wu (2019) Libritts: a corpus derived from librispeech for text-to-speech. arXiv preprint. Cited by: Datasets.. K. Zheng, H. Chen, H. Ye, H. Wang, Q. Zhang, K. Jiang, H. Su, S. Ermon, J. Zhu, and M. Liu (2025) Diffusionnft: online diffusion reinforcement with forward process. arXiv preprint. Cited by: Introduction. Major funding support from\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "ae62c58e-653f-4e16-8918-4e1af589ac7f", "uuid": "5616c06e-bd5f-486c-8fcd-5934c0597b32", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T11:11:25.219Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "content": "GROW: Group-Relative Advantage-Weighted On-Policy Reinforcement Learning of Autoregressive-Diffusion Text-to-Speech Model Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2608.03215v1 [eess.AS] 04 Aug 2026 GROW: Group-Relative Advantage-Weighted On-Policy Reinforcement Learning of Autoregressive-Diffusion Text-to-Speech Model Guanrou Yang1,2, Tian Tan1, Qian Chen4, Ziyang Ma1,2, Yakun Song1,2, Zhikang Niu1,2, Qi Chen1,2, Wenming Tu1, Haitao Li2,5, Shan Yang3, Xie Chen1,2\\corresponding Abstract Reinforcement learning for flow-matching text-to-speech is complicated by deterministic ODE sampling: trajectory-level policy-gradient methods typically convert the ODE into an SDE and track per-step likelihood ratios, introducing stochastic perturbations and substantial overhead. We propose GROW, a group-relative advantage-weighted on-policy RL method that acts directly on the standard flow-matching objective. For each prompt, GROW samples a group of on-policy utterances, separately standardizes intelligibility and speaker-similarity rewards within the group, and combines them to reweight flow-matching regression. A Wasserstein-2 velocity penalty anchors the updated model to a frozen pretrained reference. A group-mean reward baseline is introduced to convert reward weighting into advantage weighting. For strong pretrained TTS models with concentrated rewards, positive exponential weighting is dominated by reward-agnostic self-imitation, whereas a zero-mean signed advantage preserves effective within-group credit assignment. Instantiated on DiTAR and evaluated on LibriSpeech and Seed-TTS EN/ZH, GROW reduces average WER from 2.0162.016 to 1.5581.558 and raises speaker similarity from 0.6760.676 to 0.7150.715 while keeping UTMOS. With 10-NFE training rollouts and 32-NFE evaluation, GROW retains comparable performance while training 2.9×2.9\\times faster than 32-NFE DiTAR-GRPO. We will open-source complete GROW codes, faithful DiTAR reproduction, and all model checkpoints. Code — https://github.com/yanghaha0908/GROW Introduction Modern text-to-speech (TTS) systems can synthesize highly natural speech and clone an unseen speaker from a short prompt. However, their pretraining objectives provide indirect supervision for deployment-relevant attributes, such as intelligibility and speaker identity. This motivates task-aware post-training of autoregressive (AR) diffusion models, which combine fine-grained acoustic modeling in continuous latent spaces with causal AR modeling of long-range sequential dependencies. Specifically, DiTAR (Jia et al. 2025) uses a causal language model (LM) to capture dependencies across continuous latent patches and a diffusion head to generate frames within each patch. This hybrid design avoids discrete codec quantization and enables strong zero-shot synthesis. However, it also creates a distinct post-training challenge: generating an utterance requires repeatedly solving a deterministic flow ODE within an autoregressive loop, making likelihood-based reinforcement learning (RL) substantially more difficult and expensive than alignment for discrete-token LMs or NAR diffusion models. RL-based post-training has become increasingly important for speech generation. Most prior efforts act on discrete codec LMs. Seed-TTS compares REINFORCE, PPO, and DPO under rewards for recognition, speaker similarity, and emotion, while DiffRO makes token-level rewards differentiable through Gumbel–Softmax and a learned multi-task reward model (Anastassiou et al. 2024; Gao et al. 2025a; Du et al. 2025). GLM-TTS employs GRPO with multiple rewards (Cui et al. 2025), and a recent empirical study compares and combines GRPO with DiffRO in an audio LM training framework (Gao et al. 2025b). These studies demonstrate the benefits of post-training for codec-LM TTS. Alignment of continuous generators has followed several routes. ARDM-DPO fine-tunes DiTAR from offline preference pairs, while VGPO learns a causal value model whose gradient updates the model (Liu et al. 2026c, b). F5R-TTS constructs a probabilistic flow-matching TTS model for GRPO, and FlowTTS-GRPO introduces Flow-GRPO to TTS (Sun et al. 2025; Wang et al. 2026; Liu et al. 2026a). The latter route also extends to text-to-audio generation in Resonate (Li et al. 2026b). A complementary line of work, developed largely for image, directly incorporates reward signals into the forward flow-matching regression used for pretraining. ORW-CFM-W2 applies exponential reward weighting with Wasserstein-2 regularization. DiffusionNFT contrasts positive and negative synthesis to derive an implicit policy-improvement direction. AWM retains the standard score/flow-matching objective used in pretraining and reweights each sample’s loss by its advantage. FlowAWR regresses towards an advantage-rectified velocity field (Fan et al. 2025; Zheng et al. 2025; Xue et al. 2025; Fu et al. 2026). This forward-process paradigm begins to transfer to speech. SwanVoice applies DiffusionNFT post-training to its flow-matching generator, while dots.tts applies the reward-free SOAR self-correction strategy to the flow-matching head of a continuous AR model (Li et al. 2026a; Lian et al. 2026). The two paradigms address the likelihood bottleneck differently. Flow-GRPO makes policy gradients tractable by converting the sampling ODE into an SDE, but requires trajectory storage and per-step computation of policy likelihood ratios and divergences to the frozen reference, costs that recur for every generated patch in DiTAR. MixGRPO and CPS reduce the overhead and accumulated noise, yet retain stochastic sampling and trajectory-level optimization (Li et al. 2025; Wang and Yu 2025). Direct reward-weighted flow-matching regression avoids this machinery, but positive exponential weighting poses a different challenge for strong pretrained TTS models. Bounded recognition and speaker-similarity rewards are often tightly clustered within a prompt group, causing a shared self-imitation component to dominate the weak reward contrast. This regime calls for a weighting scheme that amplifies subtle within-prompt differences while accommodating heterogeneous reward scales. We introduce GROW, a group-relative, advantage-weighted on-policy RL algorithm for flow-matching TTS. We apply it to DiTAR, an AR diffusion TTS model, but it can also be applied to conventional flow-matching TTS models such as F5-TTS (Chen et al. 2025). For each prompt, GROW samples a group of utterances from the current policy and scores them for intelligibility and speaker similarity. It normalizes each reward within the group, combines them into a signed group-relative advantage, and uses this advantage to reweight the standard flow-matching loss. A Wasserstein-2 distance loss anchors the updated velocity field to the frozen pretrained model. This simple procedure preserves deterministic ODE sampling and requires neither ODE-to-SDE conversion nor per-step likelihood evaluation. On LibriSpeech-PC test-clean and the English and Chinese Seed-TTS test sets, GROW improves both target criteria while maintaining UTMOS at the pretrained level. Averaged over the three test sets, it reduces WER from 2.0162.016 to 1.5581.558 with a 0.4580.458 absolute reduction and raises speaker similarity from 0.6760.676 to 0.7150.715 with a 0.0390.039 absolute gain, surpassing those of DiTAR-GRPO baseline. GROW is also training-efficient. With 10-NFE rollouts, it achieves comparable performance to its 32-NFE setting while training 2.9×2.9\\times faster than the 32-NFE DiTAR-GRPO baseline. All models are evaluated with 32 NFE. At matched training-rollout NFE, GROW trains about 1.1×1.1\\times faster than DiTAR-GRPO. Our contributions are summarized as follows: • We propose a concise and effective on-policy RL method that directly applies signed group-relative advantage weighting to flow-matching regression with a Wasserstein-2 reference anchor. • We introduce a group-mean reward baseline to turn reward weighting into advantage weighting, then replace positive exponential weights with a signed, group-normalized advantage for effective credit assignment. • We demonstrate consistent gains and superior training efficiency over Flow-GRPO on three multilingual zero-shot benchmarks and validate the effects of rollout NFE, CFG, reward weighting and composition. Algorithm 1 GROW: Group-Relative Advantage-Weighted On-Policy RL of AR-Diffusion TTS model Input: pretrained DiTAR θpre\\theta_{\\mathrm{pre}}; dataset 𝒟={(xref,yref,y)}\\mathcal{D}=\\{(x^{\\mathrm{ref}},y^{\\mathrm{ref}},y)\\} of (prompt wav, prompt text, target text); VAE encoder ℰ\\mathcal{E}. Parameter: group size GG; W2 weight βW​2\\beta_{W2}; reward weights λsim,λwer\\lambda_{\\mathrm{sim}},\\lambda_{\\mathrm{wer}}; learning rate η\\eta. Output: fine-tuned DiTAR θ\\theta. 1: θ←θpre\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta_{\\mathrm{pre}}; θref←θpre\\theta_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\leftarrow\\theta_{\\mathrm{pre}} (frozen) 2: while not converged do 3: draw (xref,yref,y)∼𝒟(x^{\\mathrm{ref}},y^{\\mathrm{ref}},y)\\sim\\mathcal{D}, roll out GG candidates x^(k)∼πθ(⋅∣xref,yref,y)\\hat{x}^{(k)}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}\\big(\\cdot\\mid x^{\\mathrm{ref}},y^{\\mathrm{ref}},y\\big), k=1,…,Gk=1,\\ldots,G 4: compute rewards: Rsim(k)←cos⁡(SPK​(x^(k)),SPK​(xref))R^{(k)}_{\\mathrm{sim}}\\leftarrow\\cos\\!\\big(\\mathrm{SPK}(\\hat{x}^{(k)}),\\mathrm{SPK}(x^{\\mathrm{ref}})\\big); Rwer(k)←1−WER​(ASR​(x^(k)),y)R^{(k)}_{\\mathrm{wer}}\\leftarrow 1-\\mathrm{WER}\\!\\big(\\mathrm{ASR}(\\hat{x}^{(k)}),\\,y\\big) 5: compute weights: zr(k)←(Rr(k)−μr)/(σr+ε)z^{(k)}_{r}\\leftarrow\\bigl(R^{(k)}_{r}-\\mu_{r}\\bigr)\\big/\\bigl(\\sigma_{r}+\\varepsilon\\bigr) for r∈{sim,wer}r\\in\\{\\mathrm{sim},\\mathrm{wer}\\}; w(k)←λwer​zwer(k)+λsim​zsim(k)w^{(k)}\\leftarrow\\lambda_{\\mathrm{wer}}\\,z^{(k)}_{\\mathrm{wer}}+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{sim}}\\,z^{(k)}_{\\mathrm{sim}} 6: assemble training data: u(k)←[xref∥x^(k)]u^{(k)}\\leftarrow[\\,x^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\|\\hat{x}^{(k)}]; x1(k)←ℰ​(u(k))x_{1}^{(k)}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{E}(u^{(k)}) 7: sample t(k)∼U​[0,1]t^{(k)}\\sim U[0,1], x0(k)∼𝒩​(0,I)x_{0}^{(k)}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I); xt(k)←(1−t(k))​x0(k)+t(k)​x1(k)x_{t}^{(k)}\\leftarrow(1-t^{(k)})\\,x_{0}^{(k)}+t^{(k)}\\,x_{1}^{(k)}; v⋆(k)←x1(k)−x0(k)v^{\\star(k)}\\leftarrow x_{1}^{(k)}-x_{0}^{(k)} 8: compute loss: ℒ←⟨w(k)​∥vθ​(xt(k),t(k))−v⋆(k)∥D2⟩+βW2​⟨∥vθ​(xt(k),t(k))−vθref​(xt(k),t(k))∥D2⟩\\mathcal{L}\\leftarrow\\big\\langle\\,w^{(k)}\\,\\big\\lVert v_{\\theta}(x_{t}^{(k)},t^{(k)})-v^{\\star(k)}\\big\\rVert_{D}^{2}\\,\\big\\rangle+\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}}\\,\\big\\langle\\,\\big\\lVert v_{\\theta}(x_{t}^{(k)},t^{(k)})-v_{\\theta_{\\mathrm{ref}}}(x_{t}^{(k)},t^{(k)})\\big\\rVert_{D}^{2}\\,\\big\\rangle 9: update: θ←AdamW​(θ,∇θℒ,η)\\theta\\leftarrow\\mathrm{AdamW}\\big(\\theta,\\ \\nabla_{\\theta}\\mathcal{L},\\ \\eta\\big) 10: end while; return θ\\theta Preliminaries DiTAR: Autoregressive-Diffusion TTS We adopt DiTAR, an AR diffusion model that couples a causal LM with a diffusion Transformer to generate speech in a continuous latent space, avoiding the information loss of discrete acoustic tokens. A variational auto-encoder first maps a waveform to a sequence of latent frames x=(x1,…,xN)x=(x_{1},\\dots,x_{N}) with xi∈ℝDx_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{D}. DiTAR partitions the sequence into patches of PP consecutive frames. A causal Transformer models the dependencies across patches, while a diffusion head models the frames within each patch. Writing θ=(θa,θb)\\theta=(\\theta_{a},\\theta_{b}) for the two components, pθ​(x)=∏ipθa​(hi∣x≤i)​pθb​(xi+1:i+P∣hi),p_{\\theta}(x)=\\prod_{i}p_{\\theta_{a}}\\!\\left(h_{i}\\mid x_{\\leq i}\\right)p_{\\theta_{b}}\\!\\left(x_{i+1:i+P}\\mid h_{i}\\right), (1) where an aggregation encoder compresses each patch into a single embedding for the LM, and hih_{i} is the LM state that summarizes the history x≤ix_{\\leq i} and conditions the diffusion head on the next patch. The intra-patch generator is a bidirectional Local Diffusion Transformer (LocDiT). Given the context hih_{i}, a time embedding, and previously generated patches as prefix, LocDiT denoises the next patch under a linear flow-matching objective: with noise x0∼𝒩​(0,I)x_{0}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I), clean latent x1x_{1}, and t∼U​[0,1]t\\sim U[0,1], xt=(1−t)​x0+t​x1,ℒFM​(θ)=𝔼​[∥vθ​(xt,t)−(x1−x0)∥22]\\displaystyle x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+tx_{1},\\quad\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{FM}}(\\theta)=\\mathbb{E}\\bigl[\\lVert v_{\\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x_{0})\\rVert_{2}^{2}\\bigr] (2) At inference the model runs autoregressively over patches: for each patch it solves the probability-flow ODE, decodes sampled latents, and a stop head predicts the end of the utterance. Classifier-free guidance (CFG) is applied by intermittently dropping the conditioning during training and interpolating conditional and unconditional velocities at sampling time. RL for Flow-Matching Models Let πθ\\pi_{\\theta} denote the generative policy induced by the model over complete utterances, and let qq be the distribution of the frozen pretrained model, also serving as reference. For a scalar reward r​(⋅)r(\\cdot), the reward-maximization objective with a Kullback–Leibler penalty toward the reference is maxπθ⁡𝔼x∼πθ​[r​(x)]−1τ​KL​(πθ∥q),\\max_{\\pi_{\\theta}}\\ \\;\\mathbb{E}_{x\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}}\\big[r(x)\\big]\\;-\\;\\tfrac{1}{\\tau}\\,\\mathrm{KL}\\!\\left(\\pi_{\\theta}\\,\\|\\,q\\right), (3) where τ>0\\tau>0 is an inverse temperature trading reward against deviation from the reference. This objective has the well-known closed-form optimum π⋆​(x)∝q​(x)​exp⁡(τ​r​(x)),\\pi^{\\star}(x)\\;\\propto\\;q(x)\\,\\exp\\!\\big(\\tau\\,r(x)\\big), (4) that is, the reward-optimal distribution is the reference distribution exponentially tilted toward high reward. τ→0\\tau\\!\\to\\!0 recovers the pretrained model and larger τ\\tau concentrates mass on high-reward samples (Peters et al. 2010). For flow-matching generators, the exponential tilt in Eq. (4) can be realized through reward-weighted regression (RWR) without explicitly evaluating the model likelihood. Given on-policy samples x∼πnx\\sim\\pi_{n}, reward-weighted flow matching scales each sample’s standard regression loss by w​(x)=exp⁡(τ​r​(x))w(x)=\\exp(\\tau r(x)). Under the idealized assumption that the model perfectly fits the weighted flow-matching objective each round, the updated policy satisfies πn+1​(x)∝πn​(x)​exp⁡(τ​r​(x))\\pi_{n+1}(x)\\propto\\pi_{n}(x)\\exp\\bigl(\\tau r(x)\\bigr) (Fan et al. 2025; Peters and Schaal 2007). Thus, rewards can act directly through the original flow-matching objective without ODE-to-SDE conversion or trajectory likelihood evaluation. GROW builds on this direct-regression view, but revisits how the rollout rewards should be converted into regression weights for a strong pretrained TTS model. GROW GROW fine-tunes pretrained DiTAR by applying RL directly to its flow-matching acoustic head. Its design combines on-policy multi-objective rewards, a signed group-normalized advantage that replaces positive exponential weighting, and Wasserstein-2 distance anchoring to the frozen pretrained model. Algorithm 1 summarizes the full procedure, and the following subsections detail these components. On-Policy Rollouts and Multi-Objective Rewards Each optimization step draws one example (xref,yref,y)(x^{\\mathrm{ref}},y^{\\mathrm{ref}},y) —prompt waveform, its transcript, and target text—and generates a group of GG candidates {x^(k)}k=1G\\{\\hat{x}^{(k)}\\}_{k=1}^{G} from the current policy. Each rollout uses different sampling noise to form diverse continuations of the same prompt. Since each rollout is sampled from the current model, training remains on-policy and the data distribution evolves with the policy throughout optimization. Each candidate is scored on two axes that matter for zero-shot TTS, speaker similarity to the prompt and intelligibility of the target text, using Rsim(k)=cos⁡(SPK​(x^(k)),SPK​(xref))R_{\\mathrm{sim}}^{(k)}=\\cos\\!\\bigl(\\mathrm{SPK}(\\hat{x}^{(k)}),\\mathrm{SPK}(x^{\\mathrm{ref}})\\bigr) and Rwer(k)=1−WER​(ASR​(x^(k)),y)R_{\\mathrm{wer}}^{(k)}=1-\\mathrm{WER}\\!\\bigl(\\mathrm{ASR}(\\hat{x}^{(k)}),y\\bigr), where SPK\\mathrm{SPK} is a speaker encoder and ASR\\mathrm{ASR} a speech recognizer. Both reward models are frozen. From Reward Weighting to Advantage Weighting In policy-gradient RL, subtracting a baseline that is independent of the sampled action leaves the expected policy gradient unchanged, while an appropriate baseline can reduce the variance of its Monte Carlo estimate (Williams 1992; Sutton et al. 1999). Scoring each generated sample by the exponential of its own reward, w​(x)=exp⁡(τ​r​(x))w(x)=\\exp(\\tau r(x)), is the classical RWR form, which realizes the tilted optimum (4). To adapt to RL fine-tuning of a pretrained TTS model, we begin by introducing a reward baseline. Centering the reward by a constant baseline, r​(x)→r​(x)−br(x)\\!\\to\\!r(x)-b, leaves the tilted optimum (4) unchanged, for the constant factor e−τ​be^{-\\tau b} is absorbed into the normalizer. A natural and widely used baseline is the expected return under the current policy, which in group-relative methods is estimated by the group mean (Shao et al. 2024). In our bandit-style setting, where a prompt is a state, and each rollout is an action, the expected return under the current policy is naturally estimated by the group mean R¯=1G​∑kR(k)\\bar{R}=\\tfrac{1}{G}\\sum_{k}R^{(k)}. Centering the reward therefore turns the reward-weighted form into the advantage-weighted form w(k)=exp⁡(τ​A(k)),A(k)=R(k)−R¯,w^{(k)}=\\exp\\!\\big(\\tau\\,A^{(k)}\\big),\\qquad A^{(k)}=R^{(k)}-\\bar{R}, (5) removing the reward-scale factor exp⁡(τ​R¯)\\exp(\\tau\\bar{R}) that otherwise multiplies the update. From Exponential to Linear Group-Relative Advantage We now specialize (5) to the reward statistics of TTS. Since the strong pretrained policy already achieves high speaker similarity and low WER, both bounded rewards are near saturation and highly concentrated, yielding small advantages A(k)A^{(k)}. A first-order Taylor expansion of the exponential weight about A(k)=0A^{(k)}=0, exp⁡(τ​A(k))=1+τ​A(k)+O​((τ​A(k))2),\\exp\\!\\big(\\tau A^{(k)}\\big)=1+\\tau A^{(k)}+O\\!\\big((\\tau A^{(k)})^{2}\\big), (6) separates the weight into a constant term of 11 and a linear term τ​A(k)\\tau A^{(k)}. Write the advantage-weighted loss over the group as ℒaw=∑kw(k)​ℓ(k)\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{aw}}=\\sum_{k}w^{(k)}\\ell^{(k)}, where ℓ(k)\\ell^{(k)} is the flow-matching loss of rollout kk. Decomposing the weight into its group mean and deviation, w(k)=w¯+δ(k)w^{(k)}=\\bar{w}+\\delta^{(k)} with ∑kδ(k)=0\\sum_{k}\\delta^{(k)}=0, ℒaw=w¯​∑kℓ(k)⏟self-imitation+∑kδ(k)​ℓ(k)⏟reward-contrast.\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{aw}}=\\underbrace{\\bar{w}\\,{\\textstyle\\sum_{k}}\\ell^{(k)}}_{\\text{self-imitation}}\\;+\\;\\underbrace{{\\textstyle\\sum_{k}}\\delta^{(k)}\\ell^{(k)}}_{\\text{reward-contrast}}. (7) The first term reduces to ordinary flow matching on the policy’s own rollouts, which is self-imitation. Since the shared weight w¯\\bar{w} ignores reward differences, it provides no within-group credit assignment and merely reinforces the current policy. Only the reward-contrast term moves probability mass, pulling toward above-average rollouts and, when the weight is signed, pushing away from below-average ones. For a strictly positive weight such as exp⁡(τ​A)\\exp(\\tau A), the mean w¯\\bar{w} stays of order one while the contrast deviations scale as δ(k)∝τ​A(k)\\delta^{(k)}\\!\\propto\\!\\tau A^{(k)}. The ratio of reward signal to self-imitation is therefore of order τ​σ\\tau\\sigma, where σ\\sigma is the within-group advantage standard deviation. In the concentrated TTS regime, σ\\sigma is small, so unless τ\\tau is enlarged, the update is dominated by self-imitation and provides little reward signal. Enlarging τ\\tau is not a robust fix, for the useful magnitude scales with the unknown and drifting σ\\sigma, so a fixed temperature does not transfer across different prompts or training steps. These observations point to a weight that is centered, signed, and normalized by the group scale—the linear group-relative advantage, w(k)=A(k)/σ=(R(k)−R¯)/(std⁡(R)+ε).w^{(k)}=A^{(k)}\\big/\\sigma=\\bigl(R^{(k)}-\\bar{R}\\bigr)\\big/\\bigl(\\operatorname{std}(R)+\\varepsilon\\bigr). (8) The linear advantage weight A(k)/σA^{(k)}/\\sigma is the leading-order direction of the exponential advantage weight (5) when the within-group advantages are small. It is obtained from exp⁡(τ​A(k))\\exp(\\tau A^{(k)}) by four steps: (i) replacing the exponential by the leading, linear term 1+τ​A(k)1+\\tau A^{(k)} of its Taylor expansion; (ii) dropping the self-imitation constant 11; (iii) folding the positive scalar τ\\tau into the learning rate; and (iv) normalizing by the group standard deviation σ\\sigma. The resulting weight adopts the same group-normalized advantage estimator of GRPO, and is applied directly to flow-matching regression. It is zero-mean, so its self-imitation component vanishes and the update is almost purely reward-contrast. Its signed values pull the model toward above-average candidates and away from below-average ones. Its normalization makes the weight scale independent of the raw reward range, avoiding the need to tune τ\\tau against the unknown and drifting σ\\sigma. The same normalization makes multi-objective rewards easy to combine. Speaker similarity and WER have different scales and variances, so a weighted sum of raw rewards would let the higher-variance stream dominate the advantage. We therefore standardize each stream within the group and then fuse them, w(k)=∑r∈{sim,wer}λr​zr(k),zr(k)=Rr(k)−μrσr+ε.w^{(k)}=\\!\\!\\sum_{r\\in\\{\\mathrm{sim},\\mathrm{wer}\\}}\\!\\!\\lambda_{r}\\,z^{(k)}_{r},\\quad z^{(k)}_{r}=\\frac{R^{(k)}_{r}-\\mu_{r}}{\\sigma_{r}+\\varepsilon}. (9) where μr,σr\\mu_{r},\\sigma_{r} are the group mean and standard deviation of {Rr(k)}k=1G\\{R^{(k)}_{r}\\}_{k=1}^{G}. Following the dynamic-sampling strategy of DAPO (Yu et al. 2026), we discard and redraw any group with zero reward variance (σsim+σwer=0\\sigma_{\\mathrm{sim}}\\!+\\!\\sigma_{\\mathrm{wer}}=0), which carries no gradient. Advantage-Weighted Flow Matching with Reference Anchoring Each rollout is reassembled into a training example for the loss. The waveform u(k)=[xref∥x^(k)]u^{(k)}=[\\,x^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\|\\hat{x}^{(k)}] prepends the prompt to the generated audio. Extracting VAE of waveform gives the target latents x1(k)=ℰ​(u(k))x_{1}^{(k)}=\\mathcal{E}(u^{(k)}). We then sample a flow-matching time t(k)∼U​[0,1]t^{(k)}\\sim U[0,1] and noise x0(k)∼𝒩​(0,I)x_{0}^{(k)}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I) per rollout, and form the interpolant xt(k)x_{t}^{(k)} at time t(k)t^{(k)}, xt(k)=(1−t(k))​x0(k)+t(k)​x1(k),v⋆(k)=x1(k)−x0(k).x_{t}^{(k)}=(1-t^{(k)})\\,x_{0}^{(k)}+t^{(k)}\\,x_{1}^{(k)},v^{\\star(k)}=x_{1}^{(k)}-x_{0}^{(k)}. (10) The final objective is: ℒ=\\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}= ∑kw(k)​∥vθ​(xt(k),t(k))−v⋆(k)∥2\\displaystyle\\sum_{k}w^{(k)}\\,\\big\\lVert v_{\\theta}(x_{t}^{(k)},t^{(k)})-v^{\\star(k)}\\big\\rVert^{2} (11) +βW2​∑k∥vθ​(xt(k),t(k))−vθref​(xt(k),t(k))∥2,\\displaystyle+\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}}\\sum_{k}\\big\\lVert v_{\\theta}(x_{t}^{(k)},t^{(k)})-v_{\\theta_{\\mathrm{ref}}}(x_{t}^{(k)},t^{(k)})\\big\\rVert^{2}, where the first term is the advantage-weighted flow-matching loss, while the second uses the squared discrepancy between the policy and reference velocities evaluated at the same noised input xt(k)x_{t}^{(k)} as a tractable upper bound on their Wasserstein-2 distance. The signed weights encourage fitting above-average candidates while suppressing below-average ones. However, negative weights can drive the corresponding squared regression errors to grow without bound, thereby destabilizing optimization. The reference penalty mitigates this tendency by keeping the fine-tuned velocity field close to the pretrained model, with βW2\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}} controlling the trade-off. Only the policy parameters θ\\theta receive gradients. The rollouts, rewards, and reference velocities are all computed without gradients. Method NFE Speed-up LibriSpeech test-clean Seed-TTS EN Seed-TTS ZH Avg. Δ\\Delta vs. Pretrain WER↓\\downarrow SIM↑\\uparrow UTMOS↑\\uparrow WER↓\\downarrow SIM↑\\uparrow UTMOS↑\\uparrow WER↓\\downarrow SIM↑\\uparrow UTMOS↑\\uparrow WER↑\\uparrow SIM↑\\uparrow UTMOS↑\\uparrow Pretrain – – 2.373 0.648 4.30 2.406 0.663 3.95 1.269 0.717 3.14 – – – DiTAR-GRPO 32 1×\\times 2.149 0.674 4.29 2.017 0.688 3.91 1.144 0.736 2.97 0.246 0.023 -0.07 10 2.6×\\times 2.332 0.683 4.31 1.728 0.699 3.97 1.180 0.738 2.98 0.269 0.030 -0.04 5 4.0×\\times 2.393 0.665 4.21 1.873 0.688 3.93 1.194 0.730 2.91 0.196 0.018 -0.11 GROW 32 1.1×\\times 1.868 0.701 4.33 1.750 0.703 4.01 1.000 0.743 3.13 0.477 0.040 0.03 10 2.9×\\times 1.927 0.701 4.33 1.763 0.702 4.00 0.983 0.742 3.12 0.458 0.039 0.02 5 4.3×\\times 1.992 0.697 4.33 1.721 0.702 4.04 1.096 0.742 3.19 0.413 0.038 0.06 Table 1: Zero-shot TTS performance and training efficiency of GROW compared with DiTAR-GRPO baseline, applied on the same pretrained DiTAR model and evaluated on three test sets. We vary the rollout NFE during training, while fixing the inference NFE at 3232 for all models. We use NFE=10{=}10 as the default setting for the main experiments. “Speed-up” reports training wall-clock speed relative to DiTAR-GRPO with rollout NFE=32{=}32. The “Avg. Δ\\Delta vs. Pretrain” block reports the absolute improvement over the pretrained model, averaged over the three test sets (higher is better in every column). The best value in each column is set in bold. Experiments Experimental Setup Datasets. For pre-training, we use the English and Chinese portions of Emilia corpus (He et al. 2024), comprising ∼\\sim 95K hours of speech. For RL, we assemble a balanced multilingual prompt set of 30K utterances drawn in equal parts from three sources: 10K English and 10K Chinese utterances sampled from Emilia, and 10K English utterances sampled from LibriTTS train-clean-100 (Zen et al. 2019). Emilia utterances are retained only when their DNSMOS exceeds 3.23.2. Every prompt is constrained to a duration between 1.51.5 and 1515 seconds. Each training instance couples a prompt waveform and its transcript with a target text sampled randomly from the same dataset. The length of target text varies from short to long, spanning a range of synthesis difficulties. Pre-training Configurations. We use Semantic-VAE (Niu et al. 2025) for continuous speech representation that encodes 1616 kHz waveforms into 40Hz 6464-dimensional latents. A six-layer transformer aggregation encoder then processes each patch of P=4P{=}4 consecutive latents into a single vector, which is fed as one input token to the causal AR backbone. The backbone is a Qwen3-0.6B Transformer (Yang et al. 2025) with 2828 layers and a hidden size of 10241024, initialized from the released weights. The input text is tokenized using Qwen3-0.6B tokenizer. The LocDiT is a six-layer transformer with a model dimension of 10241024 and 120M parameters. The number of historical patch is set to 1. The causal LM output is dropped with probability 0.10.1 to enable CFG at sampling time. From the causal LM output, a lightweight linear stop head makes a binary continue-or-stop decision, trained with a cross-entropy loss. We optimize with AdamW optimizer, and the learning rate increases linearly from 0 to a peak rate of 1e-4 over the first 10k steps, and then decays linearly to zero during the remaining training time. The checkpoint at 200200K updates serves as both the initialization and the frozen reference qq for the RL stage. RL Fine-tuning Configurations. We fine-tune the 200200K-step pre-trained model with the objective of Eq. (11). Each optimization step draws a group of G=8G\\!=\\!8 on-policy rollouts. The two standardized reward streams of Eq. (9) are fused with equal weights λwer=λsim=1\\lambda_{\\mathrm{wer}}\\!=\\!\\lambda_{\\mathrm{sim}}\\!=\\!1, and the W2 reference anchor of Eq. (11) is weighted by βW2=0.025\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}}\\!=\\!0.025. We use AdamW with a constant learning rate of 2×10−62\\times 10^{-6}. The batch size is set to 128. The RL stage runs for several hundred updates. Both on-policy rollout and final evaluation use the same sampling procedure. Specifically, we use an Euler solver, adopt the sway sampling schedule of F5-TTS with a coefficient of −1-1, and apply CFG. The only difference lies in the number of function evaluations(NFEs), which is 1010 during rollout and 3232 at evaluation. Note that the flow-matching regression loss in Eq. (11) is computed using the conditional velocity prediction alone without CFG. Evaluation Metrics. Following standard practice, we evaluate on the LibriSpeech-PC test-clean subset proposed in F5-TTS, including 1,127 audio clips, and the Seed-TTS test sets, comprising 1,0881{,}088 English and 2,0202{,}020 Chinese prompt–target pairs. We report WER for content consistency computed by Whisper-large-v3 (Radford et al. 2023) and Paraformer-zh (Gao et al. 2022), speaker similarity (SIM) for timbre fidelity via cosine similarity between the WavLM-large-based speaker embeddings (Chen et al. 2022) of the generated and prompt speech, and UTMOS (Saeki et al. 2022) for perceptual quality. These are the same recognition and speaker models used to compute the RL reward. Experimental Result Comparison with the DiTAR-GRPO baseline. As a strong point of comparison, we implement GRPO on the same pretrained DiTAR model, which we denote as DiTAR-GRPO. We further strengthen the baseline with a mixed ODE–SDE scheme of MixGRPO (Li et al. 2025), which confines the injected stochasticity and its gradients to a short window of three denoising steps for efficiency, and the CPS method, which keeps the per-step noise level consistent with the scheduler (Wang and Yu 2025). Table 1 reports zero-shot performance across the three test sets. Both RL methods improve over the pretrained DiTAR. GROW, however, improves by a substantially larger and more consistent margin. Averaged over the three test sets, GROW lowers WER by 0.4580.458 and raises SIM by 0.0390.039, against 0.2690.269 and 0.0300.030 of DiTAR-GRPO. Crucially, GROW improves the two objectives simultaneously without trading one off against the other. It attains the highest SIM on all three test sets and the lowest WER on LibriSpeech-PC and Seed-TTS ZH. The gains are also consistent rather than concentrated on a single test set. Whereas DiTAR-GRPO improves mostly on one English set, GROW improves on all three, which indicates that the improvements are robust properties of the method rather than dataset-specific artifacts. Although UTMOS is reported only as a held-out perceptual sanity check and never enters the reward, GROW improves it whereas DiTAR-GRPO degrades it on average. We attribute this degradation to the noise injected by its SDE conversion, which our deterministic, reward-weighted update sidesteps. All values nonetheless remain within a normal range. Test set Metric Pretrain Weight exp⁡(τ​A)\\exp(\\tau A) exp⁡(τ​r)\\exp(\\tau r) A/σA/\\sigma LS-PC WER↓\\downarrow 2.373 2.223 2.361 1.927 SIM↑\\uparrow 0.648 0.646 0.647 0.701 Seed EN WER↓\\downarrow 2.406 2.102 2.251 1.763 SIM↑\\uparrow 0.663 0.662 0.666 0.702 Seed ZH WER↓\\downarrow 1.269 1.127 1.151 0.983 SIM↑\\uparrow 0.717 0.712 0.716 0.742 Avg. Δ\\Delta WER↑\\uparrow – 0.199 0.095 0.458 SIM↑\\uparrow – -0.003 0.000 0.039 Table 2: Effect of the per-rollout weighting function on zero-shot TTS performance. We compare the signed group-normalized advantage A​(x)/σA(x)/\\sigma with exponential weighting of the centered advantage exp⁡(τ​A​(x))\\exp(\\tau A(x)) and raw reward exp⁡(τ​r​(x))\\exp(\\tau r(x)). LS-PC denotes LibriSpeech test-clean. Test set Metric Pretrain Configuration (γ,v)(\\gamma,v) (1.5,vc)(1.5,v_{c}) (1.5,vg)(1.5,v_{g}) (0,vc)(0,v_{c}) LS-PC WER↓\\downarrow 2.373 1.927 2.287 1.953 SIM↑\\uparrow 0.648 0.701 0.657 0.688 Seed EN WER↓\\downarrow 2.406 1.763 2.283 1.648 SIM↑\\uparrow 0.663 0.702 0.671 0.695 Seed ZH WER↓\\downarrow 1.269 0.983 1.195 1.052 SIM↑\\uparrow 0.717 0.742 0.723 0.744 Avg. Δ\\Delta WER↑\\uparrow – 0.458 0.094 0.465 SIM↑\\uparrow – 0.039 0.007 0.033 Table 3: Effect of rollout CFG and training velocity on zero-shot TTS performance. We vary the rollout guidance scale γ\\gamma and apply the flow-matching loss to either the conditional prediction vcv_{c} or the guided prediction vgv_{g}. Rollout NFE and training efficiency. Both RL methods spend most of each training step on the on-policy rollout. Since DiTAR generates autoregressively, producing a single candidate runs a full ODE for every latent patch. Each ODE is integrated over a number of denoising steps that we treat as the rollout budget and report as the NFE column of Table 1. Besides, the LocDiT forwards twice under CFG at every step, once conditionally and once unconditionally. A rollout thus requires on the order of patch count×NFE\\text{patch count}\\times\\text{NFE} LocDiT forward passes, making it the dominant contributor to the per-step wall-clock time. Since Flow-GRPO shows coarse sampler suffices to collect on-policy data even when fine sampling is needed at test time, we reduce the rollout NFE in training while always evaluating the final model at NFE=32\\mathrm{NFE}=32. Table 1 sweeps NFE∈{32,10,5}\\mathrm{NFE}\\in\\{32,10,5\\} for both methods. For GROW, reducing the rollout NFE barely changes final quality. Cutting NFE from 3232 to 1010 shrinks the average WER reduction by only 0.0190.019, from 0.4770.477 to 0.4580.458, and cutting it to 55 shrinks it by 0.0640.064, to 0.4130.413, while speaker similarity stays essentially flat at 0.0400.040, 0.0390.039, and 0.0380.038.This robustness to lower NFE enables substantial speed-ups with only minor losses in quality. Relative to the slowest configuration, DiTAR-GRPO at NFE=32{=}32, GROW trains 1.1×1.1\\times, 2.9×2.9\\times, and 4.3×4.3\\times faster at the three settings. We adopt NFE=10{=}10 as the default for preserving essentially the full quality of NFE=32{=}32 while training 2.9×2.9\\times faster. DiTAR-GRPO tolerates a moderate reduction but breaks down under an aggressive one. Reducing its NFE to 1010 even improves both metrics slightly at a 2.6×2.6\\times speed-up, but at NFE=5{=}5 its quality falls clearly, despite a comparable 4.0×4.0\\times speed-up. At matched NFE, the two methods use identical rollout configurations, so GROW’s consistent ∼1.1×\\sim\\!1.1\\times speed advantage over DiTAR-GRPO comes entirely from the gradient-propagation stage. GROW performs a single flow-matching regression, one LocDiT forward pass per frame, but backpropagates through the full AR backbone. DiTAR-GRPO utilizes the recorded LM output and backpropagates through the LocDiT alone, but the three SDE steps it makes stochastic per patch each enter the loss with a conditional and an unconditional forward pass, resulting in roughly 6×6\\times more computation. However, the gradient stage is small compared to rollout stage that dominates the step, so the gap between the two loss computations is diluted to ∼1.1×\\sim\\!1.1\\times as we observe. GROW is also lighter on GPU memory, as DiTAR-GRPO must additionally retain the recorded transition buffer for all rollout candidates. Overall, GROW is faster, better, and more robust to a shrinking rollout budget than DiTAR-GRPO. Linear versus exponential advantage weighting. How to map reward to the per-rollout weight w​(x)w(x) is a central design choice in GROW. Table 2 ablates this mapping, comparing our signed linear advantage A​(x)/σA(x)/\\sigma against its two exponential ancestors exp⁡(τ​A​(x))\\exp(\\tau A(x)) and exp⁡(τ​r​(x))\\exp(\\tau r(x)). The signed advantage is far more effective than either exponential weight. Averaged over the three test sets, it lowers WER by 0.4580.458 and raises SIM by 0.0390.039. The two exponential weights achieve a much smaller WER reduction, of 0.1990.199 and 0.0950.095 respectively, and leave speaker similarity essentially unmoved of −0.003-0.003 and 0. This is precisely the failure mode exposed by the self-imitation/reward-contrast decomposition discussed above. The analysis applies to both weighting forms, for we implement exp⁡(τ​r)\\exp(\\tau r) with a group softmax, equivalent to the renormalized exp⁡(τ​A)\\exp(\\tau A). The weighted loss is dominated by the self-imitation term, which reinforces the current policy’s own rollouts without ranking them. The reward-contrast term becomes negligible, and the metrics barely move. Enlarging τ\\tau is not a robust remedy, for σ\\sigma is unknown and drifting. We swept the temperature τ\\tau together with the W2 anchor weight βW2\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}}, and none of the settings lifted speaker similarity above the pretrained model. The signed, zero-mean advantage with unit-variance normalization escapes this bind. We adopt A​(x)/σA(x)/\\sigma as the default weight throughout. Rollout CFG and training velocity. GROW uses CFG for on-policy rollout, sampling with the guided velocity vg=vc+γ​(vc−v∅)v_{g}=v_{c}+\\gamma(v_{c}-v_{\\varnothing}), where v∅v_{\\varnothing} denotes the unconditional prediction obtained by zeroing the context. During training, we apply the advantage-weighted flow-matching loss to the conditional prediction vcv_{c} rather than vgv_{g}. This choice focuses the RL update on conditional generation, without explicitly optimizing the auxiliary unconditional prediction used only for CFG. It also avoids the additional unconditional forward pass required to construct vgv_{g} in the loss. Table 3 probes two alternative choices: whether CFG is used during rollout, and whether the regression loss is applied to the conditional prediction vcv_{c} or the guided prediction vgv_{g}. Counterintuitively, switching from vcv_{c} to vgv_{g} makes the method nearly ineffective: the average WER reduction falls from 0.4580.458 to 0.0940.094 and the SIM gain from 0.0390.039 to 0.0070.007, leaving only marginal improvements in both metrics across all test sets. We therefore use conditional prediction vcv_{c} in loss throughout our experiments. With this choice fixed, the rollout guidance scale γ\\gamma shifts the balance between intelligibility and speaker fidelity. Disabling CFG at γ=0\\gamma{=}0 so that rollouts are drawn from pure conditional distribution yields a slightly larger WER reduction (0.4650.465 vs. 0.4580.458) but a smaller SIM gain (0.0330.033 vs. 0.0390.039). Guiding the rollout at γ=1.5\\gamma{=}1.5 instead concentrates the candidates toward the conditioning, sharpening the speaker adherence that SIM measures, and gives the best speaker similarity. We finally adopt γ=1.5\\gamma{=}1.5, as it maximizes speaker similarity while preserving intelligibility, whereas γ=0\\gamma{=}0 is an equally attractive efficiency-oriented alternative that additionally halves the rollout cost, skipping the unconditional forward pass at every denoising step. η\\eta βW2\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}} LS-PC Seed EN Seed ZH Avg. Δ\\Delta WER↓\\downarrow SIM↑\\uparrow WER↓\\downarrow SIM↑\\uparrow WER↓\\downarrow SIM↑\\uparrow WER↑\\uparrow SIM↑\\uparrow 2​e−62\\mathrm{e}^{-6} 0 2.31 0.64 2.23 0.66 1.11 0.71 0.134 -0.003 0.025 1.93 0.70 1.76 0.70 0.98 0.74 0.458 0.039 0.05 2.13 0.69 1.89 0.69 0.99 0.74 0.344 0.030 0.1 1.98 0.67 1.84 0.69 1.15 0.73 0.360 0.020 1 2.37 0.65 2.28 0.67 1.19 0.72 0.068 0.004 1​e−61\\mathrm{e}^{-6} 0.025 1.98 0.69 1.87 0.70 1.11 0.74 0.362 0.035 5​e−65\\mathrm{e}^{-6} 0.1 2.14 0.67 2.03 0.68 1.13 0.73 0.251 0.017 1​e−51\\mathrm{e}^{-5} 0.1 2.30 0.67 2.00 0.68 1.05 0.73 0.231 0.018 Table 4: Effect of the learning rate η\\eta and W2-anchor weight βW2\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}} on zero-shot TTS performance. Test set Metric Pretrain Reward WER SIM WER+SIM LS-PC WER↓\\downarrow 2.373 1.958 2.291 1.927 SIM↑\\uparrow 0.648 0.649 0.704 0.701 Seed EN WER↓\\downarrow 2.406 1.646 1.963 1.763 SIM↑\\uparrow 0.663 0.663 0.706 0.702 Seed ZH WER↓\\downarrow 1.269 1.087 1.047 0.983 SIM↑\\uparrow 0.717 0.720 0.747 0.742 Avg. Δ\\Delta WER↑\\uparrow – 0.452 0.249 0.458 SIM↑\\uparrow – 0.001 0.043 0.039 Table 5: Effect of reward composition on zero-shot TTS performance. We compare intelligibility-only, speaker-similarity-only, and combined rewards. Learning rate and W2-anchor strength. Two important knobs in GROW are the learning rate η\\eta and the reference-anchor weight βW2\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}}, which controls how tightly the policy is tethered to the frozen pretrained model. With η\\eta fixed at 2​e−62\\mathrm{e}{-}6, the overall performance is best at an intermediate anchor strength. Removing the anchor at βW2=0\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}}{=}0 yields only modest WER gains and is the only configuration that fails to improve speaker similarity, suggesting that the unchecked signed advantage drifts off the pretrained manifold rather than concentrating on high-reward behavior. Conversely, an over-strong anchor at βW2=1\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}}{=}1 instead pins the policy so tightly that it barely moves, sharply reducing the gains on both metrics. The optimum lies in between at βW2=0.025\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}}{=}0.025, which attains both the largest WER reduction and the largest SIM gain, while the intermediate values 0.050.05 and 0.10.1 form a similar but clearly lower plateau. This confirms that GROW benefits from a light but nonzero reference anchor, which stabilizes training without impeding policy improvement. At the optimal anchor βW2=0.025\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}}{=}0.025, halving the learning rate to 1​e−61\\mathrm{e}{-}6 preserves training stability but yields smaller gains, indicating the updates are too conservative to move the model sufficiently. At the looser anchor βW2=0.1\\beta_{\\mathrm{W2}}{=}0.1, raising the learning rate to 5​e−65\\mathrm{e}{-}6 and then 1​e−51\\mathrm{e}{-}5 steadily shrinks the WER reduction, indicating that overly aggressive updates move the model beyond the neighborhood preserved by the reference anchor. The useful learning rate is thus an intermediate value that requires tuning. Taken together, the two optima confirm the signed advantage weight would diverge on its own under the squared flow-matching loss and is kept stable by the reference anchor and a small learning rate. Reward design. We next isolate the contribution of the two reward streams by training with three reward configurations: intelligibility alone, speaker similarity alone, and the two combined. Table 5 reports the result. Each single-objective reward produces a substantial gain on its own axis without sacrificing the other. And the coupling between the two axes is not symmetric. Rewarding intelligibility alone yields a large WER reduction of 0.452, while holding speaker similarity at the pretrained level. Rewarding speaker similarity alone yields a marked similarity gain of 0.043, and also reduces WER by a wide margin of 0.249, which is more than half of the reduction achieved by the intelligibility reward. A likely reason is that steering generations toward the prompt speaker also makes them cleaner and easier to recognize, while the reverse coupling is weak. Combining the two rewards then delivers both gains at once. The joint reward matches the WER-only setting on intelligibility and keeps almost all of the SIM-only setting gains on speaker similarity, reaching essentially the same level as the strong single-objective run on each axis simultaneously. Conclusions We present GROW, an on-policy RL method that fine-tunes AR-diffusion TTS by reweighting standard flow-matching regression with a signed, group-normalized advantage and stabilizing the update with a Wasserstein-2 reference anchor. Our analysis and ablations identify the weighting rule as the central design choice. When rewards from a strong pretrained model are tightly clustered, positive exponential weights are dominated by common self-imitation, whereas the zero-mean signed advantage preserves within-group reward contrast and supports joint optimization of intelligibility and speaker similarity. Across three multilingual zero-shot benchmarks, GROW consistently improves both WER and speaker similarity, preserves perceptual quality, and outperforms a DiTAR-GRPO baseline in both effectiveness and training efficiency. These results establish direct advantage-weighted flow matching as a concise and effective alternative to trajectory-level policy gradients for flow-matching TTS generators. References P. Anastassiou, J. Chen, J. Chen, Y. Chen, Z. Chen, Z. Chen, J. Cong, L. Deng, C. Ding, L. Gao, et al. (2024) Seed-TTS: a family of high-quality versatile speech generation models. arXiv preprint. Cited by: Introduction. Y. Chen, Z. Niu, Z. Ma, K. Deng, C. Wang, J. JianZhao, K. Yu, and X. Chen (2025) F5-TTS: a fairytaler that fakes fluent and faithful speech with flow matching. In Proc.ACL, Cited by: Introduction. Z. Chen, S. Chen, Y. Wu, Y. Qian, C. Wang, S. Liu, Y. Qian, and M. Zeng (2022) Large-scale self-supervised speech representation learning for automatic speaker verification. In Proc.ICASSP, Cited by: Evaluation Metrics.. J. Cui, Z. Yang, N. Li, J. Tian, X. Ma, Y. Zhang, G. Chen, R. Yang, Y. Cheng, Y. Zhou, et al. (2025) GLM-TTS technical report. arXiv preprint. Cited by: Introduction. Z. Du, C. Gao, Y. Wang, F. Yu, T. Zhao, H. Wang, X. Lv, H. Wang, C. Ni, X. Shi, et al. (2025) Cosyvoice 3: towards in-the-wild speech generation via scaling-up and post-training. arXiv preprint. Cited by: Introduction. J. Fan, S. Shen, C. Cheng, Y. Chen, C. 